    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>If you are wondering <strong>what muscles an under desk elliptical works</strong>, the short answer is your legs, and mostly the front of your thighs. Every push of the pedal uses your quadriceps, while your hamstrings, glutes, calves, shin muscles and hip flexors take smaller, supporting roles. Your arms do nothing, and your core only holds you steady in the chair.</p>
<p>That makes a seated elliptical a lower-body endurance tool rather than a strength machine. The good news is that you can change which muscles do the most work by changing pedal direction, resistance and where your feet sit on the pedals. This guide covers each muscle group, how to emphasize it, and how a seated machine compares with a full-size elliptical.</p>

<h2>Quick Answer: What Muscles Does an Under Desk Elliptical Work?</h2>
<p>Here is how each muscle group is used and the simplest way to make it work harder. The rest of the article explains each row in more detail.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Muscle</th><th>How it's worked</th><th>How to emphasize it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Quadriceps</strong> (front of thigh)</td><td>Straightens the knee on every forward push. The main driver of the motion.</td><td>Pedal forward, raise the resistance, sit slightly farther back so your knee extends more.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Hamstrings</strong> (back of thigh)</td><td>Bend the knee and pull the pedal back through the bottom of the oval.</td><td>Pedal backward, press down and back through your heels.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Glutes</strong> (buttocks)</td><td>Help extend the hip during the push, but limited by the seated position.</td><td>Pedal backward at higher resistance, heels on the pedal, sit tall on the front edge of the chair.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Calves</strong> (gastrocnemius and soleus)</td><td>Point the foot slightly as the pedal moves away from you.</td><td>Shift weight to the balls of your feet and press through the toes.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shins</strong> (tibialis anterior)</td><td>Lift the front of the foot as the pedal comes back toward you.</td><td>Pedal backward, keep toes lightly lifted, slow the tempo.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Hip flexors</strong></td><td>Lift the thigh as each knee rises toward the desk.</td><td>Pedal with less help from momentum, at a slow and controlled pace.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Core</strong> (abs, lower back)</td><td>Minimal: keeps your torso stable against the leg motion.</td><td>Sit upright without leaning on the backrest.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> forward pedaling is quad-dominant. Backward pedaling spreads more of the work to the hamstrings, glutes and shins. Resistance makes everything harder, and foot position decides whether your calves or your heels do more.</p></div>

<h2>Quadriceps: The Main Muscles an Under Desk Elliptical Works</h2>
<p>Your quadriceps are four muscles on the front of the thigh that straighten your knee. On a seated elliptical, they push the pedal forward and down on every stride, so they do the majority of the work. If your legs feel warm or tired after a long session, it is usually right above the knee.</p>
<p>Because the knee never fully straightens, the quads work in a mid-range position for a long time. That builds muscular endurance, which is useful for stairs and long walks, but it will not build much size. Raising the resistance is the most direct way to work them harder. Models with a wide magnetic range, such as the [[amazon:deskcycle-ellipse|DeskCycle Ellipse]], give you more room to progress than budget units with only a few light settings.</p>
<h3>How to work your quads harder</h3>
<ul>
<li>Increase resistance one step at a time until you can still talk comfortably.</li>
<li>Scoot your chair back an inch or two so your knee opens up at the front of the stroke.</li>
<li>Do 1-minute faster bursts followed by 2 minutes at an easy pace.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Hamstrings: Worked More When You Pedal Backward</h2>
<p>The hamstrings run down the back of your thigh and bend the knee. When you pedal forward, they help pull the pedal back under you, but the quads still dominate. When you pedal backward, the hamstrings take on a much bigger share because they are now pulling the pedal through the hardest part of the oval.</p>
<p>Most manual under desk ellipticals pedal in both directions. A good routine is to switch direction every five minutes. It spreads the effort, gives your quads a short break, and keeps a long work session from feeling monotonous.</p>

<h2>Glutes: Is an Under Desk Elliptical Good for Glutes?</h2>
<p>This is one of the most common questions, and the honest answer is: not very. The glutes are strongest when they extend the hip from a bent position to a straight one, like standing up from a squat. In a chair, your hips stay bent at roughly 90 degrees the whole time, so the glutes only contribute a little.</p>
<p>You can bring them in more by pedaling backward at higher resistance, pressing through your heels, and sitting tall on the front third of the seat rather than slumping back. You may feel a mild burn, but if building your glutes is the goal, bodyweight squats, glute bridges or hip thrusts will do far more in ten minutes than an hour of seated pedaling.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Best for glutes?</strong> Among desk-friendly options, a compact strider you use standing, like the [[amazon:stamina-inmotion-e1000|Stamina InMotion E1000]], loads the glutes more than any seated machine because your hips extend and you carry your body weight. You will need something solid to hold for balance.</p></div>

<h2>Calves and Shins: The Lower-Leg Muscles</h2>
<h3>Calves</h3>
<p>Your calf muscles point the foot. On a seated elliptical, they work lightly as the pedal moves away from you, especially if your weight shifts toward the balls of your feet. Shifting your feet forward on the pedals so you push through the front of the foot makes them work harder.</p>
<p>The calves also act as a "muscle pump" that helps return blood from the lower legs toward the heart. That is one reason people who sit for long hours often say their legs and ankles feel less heavy when they pedal. It is a reasonable comfort benefit, but if you have swelling, circulation problems or a history of blood clots, check with your doctor before relying on any device for it.</p>
<h3>Shins (tibialis anterior)</h3>
<p>The tibialis anterior on the front of your shin lifts the front of your foot. It works as the pedal comes back toward you and your foot has to stay in contact. Backward pedaling and keeping the toes lightly lifted increase the work. If you feel a mild burn on the front of your shins in your first week, this is the muscle, and it usually adapts quickly.</p>

<h2>Hip Flexors and Core</h2>
<p>The hip flexors lift your thigh, so they work every time a knee rises toward the underside of the desk. They are not working hard, but over a long session you may notice them. People who sit all day often have tight hip flexors, and gentle movement may help them feel less stiff. It will not stretch them, though, so keep a standing hip-flexor stretch in your routine.</p>
<p>Your core plays only a small role. It stabilizes your torso so the leg motion does not rock you side to side. You can make it work a bit more by sitting upright without the backrest, but do not expect any ab training from a seated elliptical. Wobbly rolling chairs make this harder, so lock your chair in place with [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]] if it drifts.</p>

<h2>How to Target Specific Muscles on an Under Desk Elliptical</h2>
<p>You have three levers: direction, resistance and foot position. Combining them lets you build a short routine that works the whole lower leg rather than just the quads.</p>
<h3>Pedal direction</h3>
<p>Forward is quad-heavy. Backward spreads work to the hamstrings, glutes and shins. Alternate every five to ten minutes.</p>
<h3>Resistance</h3>
<p>Light resistance is fine for typing, because you will not lose focus. Save moderate or heavy resistance for calls on mute, reading or video. Our guide to the <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-with-resistance/">under desk elliptical with resistance</a> covers which models offer a wide enough range.</p>
<h3>Foot position</h3>
<p>Heels down and pressing through the back of the pedal favors the hamstrings and glutes. Weight on the balls of the feet favors the calves. Toes slightly lifted favors the shins.</p>
<h3>A 15-minute muscle-balance routine</h3>
<ol>
<li>Minutes 0–5: forward, light resistance, easy warm-up.</li>
<li>Minutes 5–8: forward, raise resistance two levels (quads).</li>
<li>Minutes 8–11: backward, same resistance, heels down (hamstrings and glutes).</li>
<li>Minutes 11–13: backward, lighter resistance, toes lifted (shins).</li>
<li>Minutes 13–15: forward, light resistance, weight on the balls of your feet (calves).</li>
</ol>
<p>For more setup advice, including chair height and mats, see <a href="/how-to-use-under-desk-elliptical/">how to use an under desk elliptical</a>.</p>

<h2>Under Desk Elliptical vs Full-Size Elliptical: Muscles Worked</h2>
<p>Many searches ask what muscles an elliptical works in general, so it helps to compare. A full-size elliptical trainer and an under desk version share the same oval foot path, but the body position changes everything.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Muscle group</th><th>Under desk (seated)</th><th>Full-size (standing)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Quadriceps</td><td>Main worker</td><td>Main worker</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hamstrings</td><td>Moderate, more when pedaling backward</td><td>Moderate to high</td></tr>
<tr><td>Glutes</td><td>Light, limited by bent hips</td><td>High, especially at higher incline</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calves</td><td>Light</td><td>Moderate, supporting body weight</td></tr>
<tr><td>Core</td><td>Minimal</td><td>Moderate, for balance and posture</td></tr>
<tr><td>Arms, shoulders, chest, back</td><td>Not worked</td><td>Worked through the moving handles</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>On a full-size machine, you stand, so your legs carry your body weight and your glutes extend the hip through a much bigger range. The moving handles add pushing and pulling for the chest, shoulders, back and arms. That is why a standing elliptical burns more calories per minute. The trade-off is that you cannot type, it needs floor space and it takes dedicated time.</p>
<p>A seated machine wins on convenience: you can rack up hours of light leg movement during work you would be doing anyway. If you are still deciding between formats, our comparison of an <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-bike/">under desk elliptical vs bike</a> explains how a circular pedal path changes the muscle and knee demands.</p>

<h2>Which Under Desk Ellipticals Work Your Muscles Best?</h2>
<p>The biggest factor is the resistance range. A smooth magnetic system with several levels lets you progress, while a motorized machine in passive mode moves your legs for you and does little muscular work. Based on specs and owner reviews, these are good picks for an active, muscle-focused session.</p>
