<p>If you have limited mobility and want the motor to help, a powered model like the [[amazon:ancheer|ANCHEER under desk elliptical]] is gentler, but pedal along with it whenever you can so your muscles still work. Read <a href="/is-an-under-desk-elliptical-good-exercise/">is an under desk elliptical good exercise</a> for more on how much work a seated machine really delivers.</p>

<h2>What You Will Feel in the First Few Weeks</h2>
<p>Most people notice the front of the thighs first. A mild, warm fatigue above the knee after 20 to 30 minutes is normal and simply means the quads are doing their job. If you pedal backward a lot in week one, you may also feel the front of your shins, because the tibialis anterior rarely works that way during a normal day.</p>
<p>That kind of muscle fatigue should fade within a day and lessen as you adapt, usually within one to two weeks. Sharp pain, pain inside the knee joint, or pain that gets worse as you pedal is different. Lower the resistance, check that your knees are not rising into the desk or twisting inward, and stop if it persists. If you have a knee, hip or back condition, ask your doctor or physical therapist which resistance and session length are safe for you.</p>
<p>Desk height matters here too. If your knees come too close to the desk at the top of the stroke, you will tend to splay them outward, which changes how the muscles load and can irritate the knees. Check your fit before you buy:</p>
[[tool:clearance]]

<h2>What an Under Desk Elliptical Won't Do for Your Muscles</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>It won't build much muscle size.</strong> The resistance is light compared with weights. Expect better endurance and less stiffness rather than bigger legs.</li>
<li><strong>It won't train your upper body.</strong> There are no handles. Add push-ups, rows or bands.</li>
<li><strong>It won't strengthen your bones much.</strong> Seated pedaling is non-weight-bearing. Walking and strength training do more for bone density.</li>
<li><strong>It won't fix posture on its own.</strong> Sitting tall helps, but the core work is minimal.</li>
</ul>
<p>None of that makes it pointless. Steady leg movement through the workday adds up, which is why we cover the broader <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-benefits/">under desk elliptical benefits</a> separately, including calories and energy. For calorie estimates by body weight, see <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-burn-calories/">does an under desk elliptical burn calories</a>.</p>

<h2>Bottom Line on the Muscles an Under Desk Elliptical Works</h2>
<p>An under desk elliptical works your quadriceps most, with lighter work for your hamstrings, glutes, calves, shins and hip flexors. Pedaling backward, pushing through your heels and raising the resistance shift more work to the back of the legs. It is an excellent tool for keeping your legs active through a long day, not a replacement for strength training.</p>
